<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Nigeria</h1><p class="st-meta">1960 CE – present · capital Abuja</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Africa's most populous country, independent from Britain in 1960, which lived through civil war and military rule before civilian rule returned in 1999.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colonial-nigeria">Colonial Nigeria</a> became independent from the United Kingdom on 1 October 1960 as the Federation of Nigeria, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abubakar-tafawa-balewa">Abubakar Tafawa Balewa</a> as prime minister and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> as nominal head of state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nnamdi-azikiwe">Nnamdi Azikiwe</a> replaced the governor-general in November 1960. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> in the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> in the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> in the west had sharply different politics and cultures.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Two military coups in 1966 ended the first republic, and in May 1967 Emeka Ojukwu declared the Eastern Region the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biafra">Biafra</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigerian-civil-war">Nigerian Civil War</a> lasted 30 months, and estimates of the dead range from one to three million. Military governments followed, apart from the civilian rule of Shehu Shagari from 1979 to 1983, and the oil boom of the 1970s made oil revenue central to the economy. Babangida's annulment of the 1993 election led to Sani Abacha's coup, and the regime hanged Ken Saro-Wiwa in 1995. A new constitution was adopted on 5 May 1999.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Olusegun Obasanjo became president on 29 May 1999, beginning the Fourth Republic. Muhammadu Buhari won in 2015, the first time an incumbent lost an election. Bola Tinubu was inaugurated on 29 May 2023 after a disputed election. Insurgency, banditry and kidnapping continue in parts of the country.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nigeria.jpg" alt="Nigeria" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Jon Harald Søby . · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nigeria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1960 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Abuja</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, Yakubu Gowon, Olusegun Obasanjo, Muhammadu Buhari, Bola Tinubu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam, Christianity, indigenous religions</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Federal Republic of Nigeria</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=nigeria" aria-label="Nigeria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4887 1839 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nigeria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5207 2095L5094 2192"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5094 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5207 2095h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5207 2095h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Abuja. <a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=nigeria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
